#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049394 :topic/id #uuid "f121c6bf-38c7-56a2-930a-199717f2bc16" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068460 :question/id #uuid "4ad21d3a-bf40-5b65-8ba6-320d853e6cff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12869" :question/problem "Find the $y$-intercept of the line that passes through the point $(-1, 4)$ and has a slope of $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line passes through the point $(-1,4)$ and its slope is $2$, we substitute $x=-1$, $y=4,$ and $m=2$ into the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty &= mx + b \\\\\n\t(4) &= (2)(-1) + b \\\\\n\t4 &= -2 + b \\\\\n\t6 &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is $b=6.$ The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,6).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc85ca17-8a08-5473-9473-dce954132bd5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Line Given a Point on the Line and Its Slope"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068461 :question/id #uuid "fdc3a6e9-080b-5e2a-b5a4-3af01c7018ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12870" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(10,10).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{10-4}{10-(-2)}=\\frac{6}{12}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=\\dfrac 1 2$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac 1 2 x + b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(10,10)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+b \\\\ 10 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10+b \\\\ 10 & =5+b \\\\ b & =10-5 \\\\ b & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=\\dfrac 1 2 x + 5.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e985ae8-05ba-590a-b504-4c84884679f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068462 :question/id #uuid "ec5bebf6-58d8-5b4d-900c-3c024628647c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12871" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line that passes through the point $(-3,2)$ and has a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the line. We know that $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ so we can write the equation of the line as $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x + b.$\n\nIt remains to find the $y$-intercept of the line. We can do that by substituting the point $(-3,2)$ into the equation and solving for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+b \\\\ (2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-3)+b \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{3}{2}+b \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the line is $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=1.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(1,0).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63461668-4f76-52a4-8115-1ab135eef9e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Intercept of a Line Given a Point and a Slope"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068463 :question/id #uuid "409dfb1f-7400-5539-9dea-6c2d1b6b7ef0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12872" :question/problem "A vertical line $l$ passes through the point $(-6,3).$ Calculate the coordinates of the $x$-intercept of $l.$" :question/worked-solution "On any vertical line, all points have the same $x$-coordinate.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $(-6,3)$ is $-6,$ so the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept is also $-6.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(-6,0).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f66989ce-aac5-5737-b60b-6ed703634cd5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intercept of a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :topic/math-academy-id 398 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051081}] :topic/title "Properties of Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"}]}
